#usda 1.0
(
    defaultPrim = "CommsAntenna"
    upAxis = "Y"
    metersPerUnit = 1
)

# Comms antenna — the ONE dish in the catalogue (doc 43).
#
# The feed aperture is this assembly's connectivity endpoint. The generic link
# kernel pairs it with every other node, applies the `link.connected` verdict
# (range, elevation, body + terrain occlusion), and publishes link state there.
# Keeping the endpoint on the rotating feed makes link geometry, RF state and the
# visible beam share the exact same physical origin. Author `lunco:link:class` /
# `maxRangeM` / `minElevationDeg` on `DishHead/LinkAperture` per instance.
#
# ── The reflector is PARAMETRIC, and it is a real paraboloid ──────────────────
#
# The surface is LATHED by the engine from the three numbers on `Reflector`, and
# `crate::lathe` regenerates it whenever one of them changes. The control-point
# net is NEVER authored alongside them: two spellings of one shape let the drawn
# contour disagree with the declared model. `y = r²/4f`, walked from a small apex
# to the rim; the u direction (the exact rational circle — 9 CVs, √2/2 diagonals,
# doubled interior knots) is GENERATED, so the rim stays genuinely round rather
# than an octagon.
#
# `LunCoLatheAPI` below is the explicit opt-in to this generator. A profile token
# without that schema is not a lathe declaration; it is an authored NurbsPatch
# with no control net and therefore has no surface to render.
#
# This geometry was the descent lander's `HighGainDish`, authored inline there and
# nowhere else while every other antenna in the project drew a FLATTENED SPHERE.
# One dish, one spelling: the lander, the base mast, the rovers and the Earth
# stations all reference this prim now.
#
# ── The two knobs, and which one to turn ──────────────────────────────────────
#
#   SIZE  → `over "YawHead" { over "DishGimbal" { over "DishHead" { double3 xformOp:scale = (s, s, s) } } }`
#           The whole head — reflector, feed arm, horn — scales together, so the
#           feed stays at the focus. This is the knob you almost always want.
#           Dish diameter = 2 · rimRadius · s = 1.16 · s metres.
#
#   SHAPE → `over "YawHead" { over "DishGimbal" { over "DishHead" { over "Reflector" { float lunco:lathe:focalLength = … } } } }`
#           f/D — how deep the bowl is. Changing `rimRadius` alone does NOT move
#           the authored feed-arm control points, so a rim change wants the arm
#           re-authored; scale does not.
#
#   MOUNT → Reference this prim as a child of the body it rides on, and place it
#           with `xformOp:translate`. That is the whole mount. The antenna owns
#           BOTH its joints and names nothing outside itself, so the same
#           component mounts on a rover, lander, tower, or ground station with no
#           host-side physics at all.
#
#           `YawJoint` names `</CommsAntenna>` — this root, a plain Xform. A joint
#           endpoint that is not itself a body resolves to the nearest ANCESTOR
#           body, so the endpoint is whatever vehicle this is parented under.
#           Parenting IS the attachment; the component cannot know, and never
#           writes, the host's path.
#
#           To mount it inert (a parked dish, a static display), either
#           `over "YawJoint" ( active = false )` to take the mechanism off the
#           stage — what `ground_station.usda` does, since it parks the elevation
#           hinge and the controller too — or
#           `over "YawJoint" { bool physics:jointEnabled = false }` to keep the
#           prim and leave it unsimulated. Both are stock USD and neither
#           requires touching this file. (`jointEnabled` is declared `bool`, NOT
#           `uniform bool`: spelling it uniform is a variability mismatch and the
#           override is silently ignored.)
#
# Examples in the tree: `structures/comms_mast.usda` (s = 3.4, mast hidden — the
# tower is the mast), `vessels/landers/descent_lander.usda` (s = 2.6, mast as the
# deck boom), `vessels/rovers/rocker_bogie.usda` (s = 0.35, stock mast).
def Xform "CommsAntenna" (
    prepend apiSchemas = ["LunCoCatalogAPI"]
    kind = "component"
    doc = "Parabolic Earth-tracking antenna mechanism — Modelica pointing controller, azimuth/elevation head, lathed reflector, feed arm and horn. Owns both its hinges — mount it by referencing it as a child of the body it rides on."
)
{
    # A mechanism, not a free-standing object: it has no body of its own and
    # yaws against whatever it is parented to. `comms_mast.usda` is the spawnable
    # tower host.
    uniform bool lunco:spawnable = false

    def Cylinder "Mast"
    {
        uniform token axis = "Y"
        double radius = 0.03
        double height = 0.8
        double3 xformOp:translate = (0, 0.4, 0)
        uniform token[] xformOpOrder = ["xformOp:translate"]
        color3f[] primvars:displayColor = [(0.7, 0.7, 0.72)]
        bool physics:collisionEnabled = false
    }

    # The rotating bodies are articulation links, not decorative transforms.
    # They deliberately have no collider: the mechanism must not change a
    # vehicle's contact shape.  A collider-less dynamic body cannot derive an
    # inertia tensor, though, so both its mass and its principal body-frame
    # inertia are authored here.  Omitting that tensor leaves an invalid link in
    # the host vehicle's articulation and lets its constraint impulses corrupt
    # the chassis solve.
    #
    # The antenna owns both hinges; the host supplies nothing but a parent and a
    # translate.
    def Xform "YawHead" (
        prepend apiSchemas = ["PhysicsRigidBodyAPI", "PhysicsMassAPI"]
    )
    {
        double3 xformOp:translate = (0, 0.8, 0)
        uniform token[] xformOpOrder = ["xformOp:translate"]
        float physics:mass = 0.8
        # Compact azimuth drive housing (about 0.18 × 0.10 × 0.18 m), expressed
        # in this body's local axes.
        float3 physics:diagonalInertia = (0.003, 0.0048, 0.003)
        bool physics:collisionEnabled = false

        def Xform "DishGimbal" (
            prepend apiSchemas = ["PhysicsRigidBodyAPI", "PhysicsMassAPI"]
        )
        {
            float physics:mass = 0.6
            # Dish, feed and elevation frame.  The X-axis value is the pitch
            # axis; the transverse values retain the reflector's larger radius.
            float3 physics:diagonalInertia = (0.036, 0.018, 0.036)
            bool physics:collisionEnabled = false

            # The elevation pivot is the pole's top, not the reflector surface.
            # This short yoke terminates at the reflector's rear apex, so the pole
            # visibly supports the dish instead of passing through it. It is part of
            # this articulated link; the authored link mass/inertia above includes
            # the yoke, dish and feed as one rigid mechanism.
            def Cylinder "DishYoke"
            {
                uniform token axis = "Y"
                double radius = 0.07
                double height = 0.10
                double3 xformOp:translate = (0, 0.05, 0)
                uniform token[] xformOpOrder = ["xformOp:translate"]
                color3f[] primvars:displayColor = [(0.24, 0.25, 0.27)]
                bool physics:collisionEnabled = false
            }

            # The head: everything that must stay in proportion with the dish.
            # Scale THIS, not the reflector, so the feed horn stays at the focus.
    #
            # Default s = 0.6 ⇒ a 0.70 m dish, the rover/lander whip class.
            def Xform "DishHead"
            {
                # The yoke ends at the reflector's apex. This is a visual/mount
                # offset within the gimbal body, not an Euler-written pose; the
                # revolute joint remains the only source of its rotation.
                double3 xformOp:translate = (0, 0.10, 0)
                double3 xformOp:scale = (0.6, 0.6, 0.6)
                uniform token[] xformOpOrder = ["xformOp:translate", "xformOp:scale"]

        # A dish is a THIN SHELL — a real reflector is paint on a skin, not a solid
        # — so it must draw from behind as well as in front. `doubleSided` is stock
        # UsdGeomGprim and says exactly that; a two-surface volume would double the
        # geometry to model a thickness nothing at this scale can resolve.
        #
        # `apexRadius` is deliberately non-zero: a ring collapsed exactly onto the
        # axis is a degenerate row whose normal is a zero cross product, and a NaN
        # normal renders as a black hole at the vertex of the dish.
                def NurbsPatch "Reflector" (
                    prepend apiSchemas = ["LunCoLatheAPI"]
                )
                {
            uniform bool doubleSided = true
            uniform token lunco:lathe:profile = "paraboloid"
            float lunco:lathe:apexRadius = 0.02
            float lunco:lathe:rimRadius = 0.58
            float lunco:lathe:focalLength = 0.35
            int vVertexCount = 4
            int vOrder = 3
            # White thermal-control paint — what a high-gain reflector is actually
            # finished in.
            color3f[] primvars:displayColor = [(0.95, 0.95, 0.93)]
            bool physics:collisionEnabled = false
                }

        # Feed arm: a NURBS curve swept into a TUBE by its authored `widths` — rim
        # to focus (f = 0.35 above the apex) in one smooth cubic.
                def NurbsCurves "FeedArm"
                {
            int[] curveVertexCounts = [4]
            int[] order = [4]
            double[] knots = [0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]
            point3f[] points = [(0.56, 0.25, 0), (0.44, 0.44, 0), (0.16, 0.47, 0), (0.0, 0.38, 0)]
            float[] widths = [0.03]
            color3f[] primvars:displayColor = [(0.75, 0.75, 0.78)]
            bool physics:collisionEnabled = false
                }

        # RF has a phase centre just ahead of the feed horn. It is the only link
        # node in this assembly: a beam, range/elevation verdict and CommsLink
        # model therefore move with the dish instead of using the mast/root as an
        # approximate endpoint. Nest the horn under it so the visual and the
        # physical endpoint have one transform, one spelling.
                def Xform "LinkAperture" (
                    prepend apiSchemas = ["LunCoLinkAPI"]
                    doc = "Dish feed phase centre: connectivity endpoint and link-beam origin."
                )
                {
            bool lunco:linkNode = true
            double3 xformOp:translate = (0.0, 0.36, 0.0)
            uniform token[] xformOpOrder = ["xformOp:translate"]

            # The feed horn at the focus, looking back into the reflector.
                    def Cylinder "FeedHorn"
                    {
                uniform token axis = "Y"
                double radius = 0.045
                double height = 0.09
                color3f[] primvars:displayColor = [(0.3, 0.3, 0.34)]
                bool physics:collisionEnabled = false
                    }

            # Dedicated communications indicator — intentionally separate from
            # navigation/route colours and from the green/red link beams. This
            # is the physical "radio powered" LED at the feed assembly, so an
            # operator can identify the communications hardware at a glance.
            # It sits just ahead of the phase centre along the feed's Y axis;
            # keeping its transverse coordinates at zero preserves the dish
            # bore-sight instead of introducing a lateral visual offset.
                    def Material "CommsIndicatorMaterial"
                    {
                token outputs:surface.connect = <PreviewSurface.outputs:surface>
                        def Shader "PreviewSurface"
                        {
                    uniform token info:id = "UsdPreviewSurface"
                    color3f inputs:diffuseColor = (0.005, 0.05, 0.012)
                    color3f inputs:emissiveColor = (0.04, 2.6, 0.30)
                    float inputs:metallic = 0.0
                    float inputs:roughness = 0.26
                    token outputs:surface
                        }
                    }

                    def Sphere "CommsIndicator" (
                        prepend apiSchemas = ["MaterialBindingAPI"]
                    )
                    {
                double radius = 0.055
                double3 xformOp:translate = (0.0, 0.085, 0.0)
                uniform token[] xformOpOrder = ["xformOp:translate"]
                rel material:binding = <CommsIndicatorMaterial>
                bool physics:collisionEnabled = false
                    }

            # The beam is a child of the physical endpoint, so its generic driver
            # resolves this LinkState rather than the antenna mount.
                    def "LinkBeam" (
                        prepend references = @lunco://components/comms/link_beam.usda@</LinkBeam>
                    )
                    {
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # Pointing mathematics is Modelica, not a scenario script. A distinct
    # environment source supplies the unit direction in THIS root's mount frame:
    # +X right, +Y up, -Z forward. EarthTracker is the sole vector→joint-angle
    # conversion; hosts never copy an axis or sign convention.
    def Xform "Environment" (
        prepend references = @lunco://components/environment/probe.usda@</EnvironmentProbe>
    )
    {
    }

    def Xform "EarthTrackerController" (
        prepend apiSchemas = ["LunCoProgramAPI"]
    )
    {
        uniform token info:implementationSource = "sourceAsset"
        uniform asset info:sourceAsset = @lunco://models/LunCo/Pointing/EarthTracker.mo@
        float inputs:earth_mount_x.connect = </CommsAntenna/Environment.outputs:earth_mount_x>
        float inputs:earth_mount_y.connect = </CommsAntenna/Environment.outputs:earth_mount_y>
        float inputs:earth_mount_z.connect = </CommsAntenna/Environment.outputs:earth_mount_z>
    }

    # ── The two hinges. BOTH live here, and nowhere else. ────────────────────
    #
    # They used to be split: elevation here, azimuth authored by each host. Every
    # host therefore wrote the same joint against a prim inside this component's
    # namespace — `AntennaYawJoint`, `CommsYawJoint`, `HighGainYawJoint`, three
    # spellings of one mechanism, each also hand-typing an anchor that had to
    # agree with a mast height it did not own. One host simply never wrote it and
    # its dish silently never tracked.
    #
    # The Modelica controller provides radians directly to the standard joint
    # port; neither joint ever writes an Euler transform.

    # AZIMUTH. `body0` is this component's ROOT — not a body — so it resolves to
    # the nearest ancestor body: whatever vehicle this antenna is parented under.
    # That is the entire mount interface, and it is why nothing here names a host.
    #
    # Both anchors are deliberately UNAUTHORED. They derive from the transform
    # hierarchy, which is the only place that knows where the head sits: the host
    # sets this component's `translate` and may override the mast's height, and a
    # typed anchor would have to be kept in agreement with both by hand — which is
    # precisely what the old per-host joints got wrong.
    def PhysicsRevoluteJoint "YawJoint"
    {
        rel physics:body0 = </CommsAntenna>
        rel physics:body1 = </CommsAntenna/YawHead>
        uniform token physics:axis = "Y"
        # Explicit port declaration: the cosim joint adapter exposes the
        # measured and commanded angle through this USD property. Without the
        # sink, the authored tracker connection is silently dropped during
        # composition.
        float inputs:angle
        float inputs:angle.connect = </CommsAntenna/EarthTrackerController.outputs:az>
    }

    # ELEVATION. `body0` is the yaw head and `body1` the elevation frame — both
    # local, both bodies, so no resolution is involved.
    def PhysicsRevoluteJoint "ElevationJoint"
    {
        rel physics:body0 = </CommsAntenna/YawHead>
        rel physics:body1 = </CommsAntenna/YawHead/DishGimbal>
        uniform token physics:axis = "X"
        point3f physics:localPos0 = (0, 0, 0)
        point3f physics:localPos1 = (0, 0, 0)
        float inputs:angle
        float inputs:angle.connect = </CommsAntenna/EarthTrackerController.outputs:el>
    }

}
